      "side": 1,
      "message": "\"The two never grow past the limit between them\" only holds in one order. A page is refused when the pages plus the charges would pass `max_bytes`, but a charge is refused only when the charges alone would. So once the heap has taken its pages, the charges can add up to `max_bytes` more. Pages are never given back, so this covers a heap that was full once and has since been emptied.\n\nMeasured on 1260691 patch set 3 with an 8-page limit (262,144 bytes), under all four counting strategies:\n\n1. Fill the heap, drop everything and call `collect_garbage()`.\n2. Intern 1,000-character names until one is refused.\n\n186 symbols went in, all in the reused pages, before `OutOfMemory`. That\u0027s 262,144 bytes of pages plus at least 186,000 bytes of name charges, 1.71 times the limit, and more once the per-entry overhead is counted.\n\nIf 1260690 makes the heap give back empty pages when a charge needs room, this sentence holds as written, and the one before it (\"never gets pages back\") changes. Otherwise the bound to state is \"up to twice `max_bytes`\".",
